  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0152 database is used to manage various entities and their relationships within a system. It contains nine tables: abilities, users, model_has_abilities, roles, tenants, assigned_roles, permissions, activity_logs, and model_has_roles. The abilities table stores information about the abilities or permissions within the system, including a unique identifier, name, title, entity ID, entity type, and scope, with the ability to store additional options in a JSON format. The users table holds information about the users, including their ID, name, email, password, and timestamps for when they were created and last updated. The model_has_abilities table links abilities to specific models, with each link having a unique combination of model type, model ID, and ability ID. The roles table contains information about the roles within the system, including a unique identifier, name, title, level, and scope, with timestamps for when they were created and last updated. The tenants table stores information about the tenants, including their ID, name, and timestamps for when they were created and last updated. The assigned_roles table links roles to entities, with each link having a unique combination of role ID, entity ID, and entity type, and optionally a restricted ID and type. The permissions table links abilities to entities, with each link having a unique ability ID, entity ID, and entity type, and a forbidden flag. The activity_logs table stores information about the activities performed within the system, including the type of loggable object, its ID, the action taken, the user who performed the action, and the timestamp when the action was performed. The model_has_roles table links roles to models, with each link having a unique combination of role ID, model type, and model ID. The abilities table has a primary key on its ID column, and the users, model_has_abilities, roles, tenants, assigned_roles, permissions, activity_logs, and model_has_roles tables also have primary keys on their ID columns. The model_has_abilities and model_has_roles tables have composite primary keys on their model ID, model type, and ability ID and role ID, model type, and model ID columns, respectively. The assigned_roles table has a foreign key constraint on its role ID column referencing the roles table's ID column, with an ON DELETE RESTRICT rule to prevent deletion of roles that are still assigned to entities. The permissions table has a foreign key constraint on its ability ID column referencing the abilities table's ID column, with an ON DELETE RESTRICT rule to prevent deletion of abilities that are still linked to entities.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing a framework-agnostic role and ability management system utilizing Laravel Eloquent models. This solution allows dynamic assignment of roles and granular abilities to users, supporting both direct and role-based permissions. It is designed to efficiently manage and cache permissions, integrate with Laravel’s authorization system, and support multi-tenancy, making it adaptable to various application contexts. The system employs a database-driven approach to role and ability management, catering to applications that require fine-grained access control.\n\nObjectives\n\n1. Create a versatile role and ability management system compatible with any application using Eloquent models.\n2. Enable dynamic assignment and management of user roles and abilities.\n3. Ensure seamless integration with Laravel’s existing authorization mechanisms.\n4. Support multi-tenancy to handle permissions across different application contexts.\n5. Optimize performance through efficient caching of permissions.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Implement and maintain the system, ensuring it integrates well with existing applications.\n- Administrators: Manage user roles and abilities, ensuring correct permissions are assigned.\n- End Users: Utilize the system within various applications, benefiting from finely controlled access.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n1. Role and Ability Management: \n   - Support both direct and role-based permissions.\n   - Allow administrators to assign roles and abilities dynamically to users.\n   - Provide a database-driven approach for managing roles and abilities.\n\n2. Permission Caching: \n   - Efficiently cache permissions to enhance system performance.\n\n3. Laravel Integration: \n   - Seamlessly integrate with Laravel’s existing authorization system for consistent application security.\n\n4. Multi-Tenancy Support: \n   - Handle permissions across different application contexts, supporting multi-tenancy.\n\n5. Activity Logging: \n   - Log activities related to assignment and changes in roles and abilities for audit purposes.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n1. Performance:\n   - Ensure adequate performance to handle a large number of permission checks efficiently.\n\n2. Scalability:\n   - Design the system to support scalability as the number of users and roles grow.\n\n3. Security:\n   - Implement robust security measures to prevent unauthorized access and permission escalation.\n\n4. Maintainability:\n   - Ensure the system is easy to maintain and extend with clear documentation and modular design.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_llama31/0152.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0152\nCREATE TABLE `abilities` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `entity_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `entity_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `scope` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `options` json DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `model_has_abilities` (\n  `model_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `model_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `ability_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`model_id`, `model_type`, `ability_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `model_has_abilities_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`ability_id`) REFERENCES `abilities` (`id`) ON DELETE RESTRICT ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `roles` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `level` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `scope` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `tenants` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `assigned_roles` (\n  `role_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `entity_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `entity_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `restricted_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `restricted_type` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`role_id`, `entity_id`, `entity_type`),\n  CONSTRAINT `assigned_roles_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`role_id`) REFERENCES `roles` (`id`) ON DELETE RESTRICT ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `permissions` (\n  `ability_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `entity_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `entity_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `forbidden` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`ability_id`, `entity_id`, `entity_type`),\n  CONSTRAINT `permissions_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`ability_id`) REFERENCES `abilities` (`id`) ON DELETE RESTRICT ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `activity_logs` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `loggable_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `loggable_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `action` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `model_has_roles` (\n  `model_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `model_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `role_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`model_id`, `model_type`, `role_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `model_has_roles_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`role_id`) REFERENCES `roles` (`id`) ON DELETE RESTRICT ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
